      "latency_ms": 2192.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the surface of Earth. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (water in rivers, oceans, rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity)\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater has several unusual and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it carries nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through ecosystems and living bodies.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and the internal temperature of living organisms.\n* **Expansion When Freezing:** Unlike most substances, water expands and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\n* **The Driver of Life:** Up to 60% of the adult human body is made of water, and about 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water (mostly oceans). \n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled through the Earth in a closed system called the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the atmosphere, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into bodies of water.\n\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with complex properties that makes existence as we know it possible."
